  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "The grid and large-load build-out continues but stops accelerating. The 63.7bn backlog converts on schedule, giving ~20% organic growth in FY2027 and low-double-digit growth in FY2028, after which utility capex and data-center interconnection grow at a high-single-digit rate that fades to 3%. Craft-labour scarcity caps conversion speed but also protects pricing, so adjusted EBITDA margin holds near the FY2026 level of 11% before easing to 10.5% as the data-center and EPC mix normalises. Tuck-in M&A continues but is treated as value-neutral.",
    "bull": "A multi-year electrification supercycle: hyperscaler and advanced-manufacturing load, transmission expansion and gas/renewable interconnection all run together, backlog keeps growing faster than revenue, and Quanta's scale in linemen and its in-house transformer and civil capabilities let it take share from regional contractors. Organic growth stays in the teens through FY2029 and margins step up to 12% on turnkey data-center and EPC work, with customer prepayments keeping working-capital needs light.",
    "bear": "Hyperscaler capex is cut and the fastest-growing part of backlog (large-load and generation EPC, mostly cancellable or deferrable) is pushed out. Revenue stalls in FY2028 and falls in FY2029-30, leaving the utility MSA base. Fixed-price exposure on large projects and under-absorbed labour take adjusted EBITDA margin back to ~9%, the pre-2025 level, and the debt taken on for 2024-26 acquisitions (goodwill and intangibles bought at peak multiples) earns less than planned. A wildfire or casualty liability event sits on top of this path and is not separately provisioned."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Quanta is valued as a cyclical specialty contractor at a record point in its cycle. Owner FCF charges SBC and the cash tax withheld on vesting awards (together ~1.1% of revenue), deducts maintenance and growth capex, and treats growth as a consumer of working capital. The model gives full credit for the backlog in FY2027-28 but does not capitalise FY2026's working-capital inflow or assume the terminal buyer pays more than 15x owner FCF. On that basis the current price implies an outcome at or beyond the bull scenario.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Terminal multiple: the 15x cap binds in the base case; each 1x is worth roughly $13/share",
      "Margin durability: each 1pp of adjusted EBITDA margin held across the whole horizon is worth roughly $45-50/share in the base case (about $27 of it in the terminal value)",
      "M&A is assumed value-neutral; if tuck-ins keep earning above the cost of capital the model understates value, and if 2024-26 deals were bought at peak multiples it overstates it",
      "Working capital: contract liabilities of 5.5bn are customer prepayments that would unwind if large-load project starts slow",
      "FY0 is a guided year, not a reported one; Q4-2026 results replace the base revenue and SBC anchors"
    ]
  },
